## Thread.Abort → Cooperative Cancellation Migration

### Pattern Classification

Your code has three patterns that break on .NET 8:

| API | Problem on .NET 8 | Replacement |
|-----|-------------------|-------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Never thrown — cleanup never runs | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token))
        {
            IsBackground = true
        };
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — migrated from ThreadAbortException handler
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — cooperative cancellation lets us
                // decide whether to continue or exit naturally.
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerThread is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signals the token — replaces Thread.Abort()

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
        {
            // Thread didn't exit in time. With cooperative cancellation,
            // this means GetNextItem/ProcessItem isn't checking the token.
            // Log a warning — you cannot force-kill a managed thread in .NET 8.
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

### Key Changes Explained

**1. `GetNextItem()` must accept the token.** This is the most critical point. If `GetNextItem()` blocks on a synchronous call (e.g., `queue.Take()`, `socket.Receive()`, `Thread.Sleep()`), cancellation won't work until it unblocks. Fix:

```csharp
// Before — blocks forever, ignoring cancellation
var item = _queue.Take();

// After — throws OperationCanceledException when token fires
var item = _queue.Take(token);              // BlockingCollection
// or
await channel.Reader.ReadAsync(token);      // System.Threading.Channels
// or
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(pollInterval);     // replace Thread.Sleep
```

**2. `Thread.Join` replaces `Thread.Sleep` + `IsAlive` check.** Your original code slept 2 seconds then checked — `Join(TimeSpan)` does the same thing atomically and returns whether the thread exited.

**3. No force-kill fallback exists.** If the thread won't stop after `Join` times out, you're stuck. The only option for truly uncooperative code is to move it to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()`.

### Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| Not passing token into blocking calls | Cancellation is **cooperative** — `Thread.Sleep`, `WaitOne()`, and synchronous I/O ignore the token. Your thread hangs. |
| Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource` | Leaks internal timers and registered callbacks. Always `Dispose()`. |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` to avoid swallowing unrelated exceptions. |
| Assuming cancellation is instant | It only fires at the next checkpoint. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `ProcessItem` if it's long-running. |
